        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Hiroshi Nishida",
          "email": "hnishida@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-11-12 22:35:54.000000000",
          "tz": -480
        },
        "subject": "LU-20730 target: limit an object table scan by nodemap",
        "message": "LU-20730 target: limit an object table scan by nodemap\n\nA scan from a client reads the whole object table of each MDT, and\nOBD_IDX_READ carries no user credentials.  With nodemaps active the\nMDT now decides by the client's nodemap:\n\n- it must be admin and have the byfid_ops role; otherwise -EPERM;\n- a tenant, a nodemap with an ID offset, then gets only the records\n  whose UID and GID are both in its ranges, as every file it creates\n  is, with the IDs mapped back to its own; a dynamic sub-nodemap,\n  which shares its parent's ranges, is refused;\n- a nodemap without an offset gets everything, so it must also be\n  trusted and have no fileset, nor its parent; otherwise -EPERM.\n\nWith nodemaps inactive nothing changes.  The ID ranges stand in for\nthe tenant's fileset: nodemap already puts every file a tenant creates\ninside them, and checking a path on the MDT would cost a lookup per\nobject.  That holds only while no other nodemap creates files with\nIDs in the tenant's ranges, which nodemap does not prevent.\n\nThe records are filtered in place after dt_index_read(), so the\nobject walk and the resume hash are unchanged.  What is dropped is\nzeroed, since the whole container goes to the client.  A container\ncan come back with no records; the client already reads on until the\nMDT says the table has ended.\n\nSigned-off-by: Hiroshi Nishida <hnishida@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:Opus-5 llm_code_and_review_tools\nTest-Parameters: ignore\nChange-Id: Idc953dc914ce533c5298a5042701733da7c88697\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
